WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.140
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.720
Today we have two extremely hard problems
on the menu.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.000
One is facial alignment and the other is 3D
facial reconstruction.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.390
For both problems, we have an image as an
input, and the output should be either a few

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:21.970
lines that mark the orientation of the jawline,
mouth and eyes, and in the other case, we

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.980
are looking for a full 3D computer model of
the face.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.120
And all this should happen automatically,
without any user intervention.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.390
This is extremely difficult, because this
means that we need an algorithm that takes

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:40.589
a 2D image, and somehow captures 3D information
from this 2D projection, much like a human

00:00:40.589 --> 00:00:41.589
would.

00:00:41.589 --> 00:00:47.440
This all sounds great and would be super useful
in creating 3D avatars for Skype calls, or

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.890
scanning real humans to place them in digital
media such as feature movies and games.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.190
That would be amazing, but, is this really
possible?

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.899
This work uses a convolutional neural network
to accomplish this, and it not only provides

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:06.080
high-quality outputs, but it creates them
in less than 10 milliseconds per image, which

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.979
means that it can process a hundred of them
every second.

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:14.619
That is great news indeed, because it also
means that doing this for video in real time

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:16.280
is also a possibility!

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.979
But not so fast, because if we are talking
about video, new requirements arise.

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:25.200
For instance, it is important that such a
technique is resilient against changes in

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.200
lighting.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.110
This means that if we have different lighting
conditions, the output geometry the algorithm

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:32.020
gives us shouldn't be wildly different.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.070
The same applies to camera and pose as well.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:39.430
This algorithm is resilient against all three,
and it has some additional goodies.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:45.039
For instance, it finds the eyes properly through
glasses, and can deal with cases where the

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:50.890
jawline is occluded by the hair, or infer
its shape when one side is not visible at

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:51.890
all.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:55.909
One of the key ideas is to give additional
instruction to the convolutional neural network

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:00.649
to focus more of its efforts to reconstruct
the central region of the face because that

00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:03.299
region contains more discriminative features.

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:07.799
The paper also contains a study that details
the performance of this algorithm.

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:12.750
It reveals that it is not only five to eight
times faster than the competition, but also

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.530
provides higher quality solutions.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:19.840
Since these are likely to be deployed in real-world
applications very soon, it is a good time

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.250
to start brainstorming about possible applications
for this.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.599
If you have ideas beyond the animation movies
and games line, let me know in the comments

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:28.599
section.

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:30.599
I will put the best ones in the video description.

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:33.950
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

